1223	\begin{lemma}[Formalization support: conditioning and restoring line defects]
1224		\label{lem:qld-line-conditioning-restoration}
1225		\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.consistencyDefect_nondegenerateLinePastingDist_le}
1226		\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.exists_combinedPoints_conditioned_line_marginal_defect_le}
1227		\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.consistencyDefect_le_nondegenerateLinePastingDist_add_mass}
1228		\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.ProjectiveSetting.combinedLineMeasurement_consistency_le_conditioned}
1229		\leanok
1230		\uses{lem:qld-point-line-marginal-bounds, lem:qld-zero-direction-mass,
1231			def:consistency, def:concrete-paired-line-measurement}
1232		Let $\mu$ be the probability that the first line in a pair of independent
1233		line-point samples has nonzero direction. For arbitrary complete
1234		measurements on directed opposite placements, conditioning on this event
1235		increases the consistency defect by at most a factor $1/\mu$, while restoring
1236		the original law gives
1237		\[
1238			\operatorname{defect}_{\mathrm{all}}
1239			\leq \mu\operatorname{defect}_{\mathrm{cond}}+\frac1{2q}.
1240		\]
1241		For the completed joint point marginals and the evaluated $Z$- and $X$-line
1242		measurements in the order required by the $X$-outer sandwich, there is a
1243		universal $C\geq1$ for which both conditioned defects are at most
1244		\[
1245			\frac{8\delta_Q+C(\eps+\sqrt\eps)}{\mu}.
1246		\]
1247		The same restoration inequality applies to the concrete paired-line
1248		measurement and the completed joint points.
1249	\end{lemma}
1250	\begin{proof}
1251		\leanok
1252		\uses{lem:qld-point-line-marginal-bounds, lem:qld-zero-direction-mass,
1253			def:consistency, def:concrete-paired-line-measurement}
1254		Restrict the finite expectation to the nondegenerate event and divide by its
1255		positive mass. The integrand is nonnegative and at most one on opposite
1256		placements, so restoring the discarded event costs at most its probability.
1257	\end{proof}
