1380	\section{Applying the classical low-degree test}
1381	\label{sec:apply-ldt}
1382	
1383	The combined point and line measurements of the previous two sections are next
1384	merged into a single measurement returning a pair of global polynomials that
1385	encode the $X$-basis and the $Z$-basis information simultaneously. This is
1386	done by encoding the pair as one polynomial in $2m+2$ variables and invoking
1387	the quantum soundness of the classical low individual degree test
1388	(Theorem~\ref{lem:ld-soundness}). The classical game, its question
1389	distribution, and the line-point distribution are defined at dimension $2m+2$
1390	only when $2m+2$ divides $q$ (Definition~\ref{def:ld-game}), a condition that
1391	admissibility of $(q,m,d)$ does not supply. The seed-bearing source route is
1392	blocked at the instantiation step for admissible parameters with $m \ge 2$.
1393	The global-pair conclusion also has the directly indexed proof below. The
1394	obstruction is recorded in Remark~\ref{rem:qld-4-7-divisibility} and
1395	analyzed, together with candidate repairs, in
1396	\cite{gap:qpbt_ld-dimension-divisibility}.
