1280	\section{Applying the classical low-degree test}
1281	\label{sec:apply-ldt}
1282	
1283	The combined point and line measurements of the previous two sections are next
1284	merged into a single measurement returning a pair of global polynomials that
1285	encode the $X$-basis and the $Z$-basis information simultaneously. This is
1286	done by encoding the pair as one polynomial in $2m+2$ variables and invoking
1287	the quantum soundness of the classical low individual degree test
1288	(Theorem~\ref{lem:ld-soundness}). The classical game, its question
1289	distribution, and the line-point distribution are defined at dimension $2m+2$
1290	only when $2m+2$ divides $q$ (Definition~\ref{def:ld-game}), a condition that
1291	admissibility of $(q,m,d)$ does not supply. The seed-bearing source route is
1292	blocked at the instantiation step for admissible parameters with $m \ge 2$.
1293	The global-pair conclusion also has the directly indexed proof below. The
1294	obstruction is recorded in Remark~\ref{rem:qld-4-7-divisibility} and
1295	analyzed, together with candidate repairs, in
1296	\cite{gap:qpbt_ld-dimension-divisibility}.
